  "query_preview": "/Through Monday/...\nIssued at 1053 PM MDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nAs of late this evening, the upper level trough is moving through \nsouthern California and Hurricane Polo is nearing the west coast of \nBaja California. The trough will slowly makes its way across the \nsoutheast U.S., eventually absorbing the remnants of Polo on \nTuesday. Moisture ahead of and with this system is anomalously high, \nleading to rain chances for the next few days. Tonight, a weak cold \nfront will move through the area bringing northerly winds and more \ndense low to mid cloud cover. The northerly winds behind the front \ncould provide enough weak upslope flow along the Palmer and southern \nFoothills for some light showers throughout the day Tuesday. \nTemperatures will be slightly cooler behind the front, with temps \ndropping into the 60s for much of the plains. \n\nThe main concern for this forecast will be the late Tuesday through \nmid Thursday time period. The upper level low is forecast to move \njust south of the four corners region early Wednesday. At the \nsurface a low is forecast to develop just ahead of the trough \nsomewhere in New Mexico. The path of this low is one of the main \ndifferences between models, with some taking it northeast into the \nTexas Panhandle and others take it more north into southeastern \nColorado. The location of this low will be very important for our \nrain chances. If the low tracks closer to the area, our chances\nfor more widespread precip will increase as well as the potential\nfor higher rain totals. If the low tracks more towards the Texas \nPanhandle and Oklahoma, the larger precip amounts will likely \nmiss our area. I will say, higher resolution models are leaning \nmore towards the surface low moving into southeastern Colorado, \nwhich increases our confidence in the higher precip amounts for \nour southern most counties. However, the northward extent of the\nheavier rainfall is still very uncertain. Right now, it looks \nlike that northward extent of heavier precip could sit somewhere \nnear Denver, which unfortunately means that the metro is one the \narea with the highest uncertainty for rain totals. \n\nWe are starting to get increasingly concerned about the Palmer \nDivide area in terms of the higher rain totals. If the low does end \nup moving over SE Colorado, strong northerly winds could provide \ndecent upslope along the Palmer and southern Foothills, which could \nnudge it towards those higher precip amounts. Because of this we \ndid extend the Flood Watch to include the southern foothills zone.\nI did consider adding Arapahoe county and Washington county to \nthe Watch as well, but we will wait until confidence increases in \nthe northward extent of the higher rain totals. Areas within the \nwatch are expected to see rainfall totals around 1.5 to 3 inches,\nwith the potential for some areas to see 4+ inches. As of the \nlatest model runs, Denver is sitting at a 50% to 60% chance of \nseeing at least 1 inch of rain late Tuesday through Thursday, \nwhich is slightly higher than what we were seeing yesterday. \n\nSnow chances in the higher terrain will peak early Thursday\nmorning as temperatures finally start to cool more aloft. Amounts\nare still forecast to be on the lower end (a couple inches at \nmost), with snow levels generally around 11,000ft. Rain chances \nwill start to taper off on Thursday as this system moves off to \nthe east. Behind the trough, we'll see weak ridging start to build\nover the area this weekend, leading to drier and warmer \nconditions. Models are showing a cold front moving through the \narea on Saturday, keeping temperatures slightly cooler and closer \nto normal for Sunday.",
